WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.180
You know, usually when we talk about a medical diagnosis or, I mean, really any kind of structural

00:05.180 --> 00:09.220
assessment, there's this built-in expectation of precision.

00:09.640 --> 00:11.880
Absolutely. It's like engineering. You want a clear answer.

00:11.980 --> 00:16.360
Right, exactly. It's like you break your arm, the x-ray shows a jagged white line on the film,

00:16.540 --> 00:18.200
and the doctor just points at it.

00:18.260 --> 00:21.260
And they say, well, there it is. That's the problem. Broken or not broken.

00:21.460 --> 00:26.500
Yeah, and it's a very comforting binary. You know, it gives us a really clear path to fix it.

00:26.600 --> 00:27.960
We crave that visibility.

00:27.960 --> 00:35.820
We really do. But then you step into almost any deeply complex system, whether that's like

00:35.820 --> 00:39.340
neurodevelopment or macroeconomics or...

00:39.340 --> 00:42.420
Or even just trying to fix legacy code in a software program.

00:42.680 --> 00:45.940
Right. And suddenly that clean x-ray machine is completely useless.

00:46.340 --> 00:50.320
You're looking at a diagnostic landscape that is just, well, it's murky.

00:50.400 --> 00:53.040
You pull one thread and three others unravel.

00:53.340 --> 00:56.140
Because you're suddenly in the realm of cascading dependencies.

00:56.140 --> 00:58.700
You can't just isolate one thing.

00:59.060 --> 01:03.760
Because the thing is actually defined by its relationship to, you know, everything else around it.

01:03.920 --> 01:04.080
Mm-hmm.

01:04.320 --> 01:10.940
And that feeling, that vertigo of stepping out of a clean binary world of isolated objects

01:10.940 --> 01:12.980
and into something infinitely more tangled,

01:13.700 --> 01:17.900
that is exactly the feeling I got when I opened up the source material for today's Deep Dive.

01:17.900 --> 01:23.920
It is quite the document. For those listening, we are looking at a dense 65-page working draft.

01:24.040 --> 01:29.300
Yeah. It's dated June 10, 2026. It's titled The Flyxion Project Map.

01:29.300 --> 01:36.220
And, um, this document claims to outline the intellectual architecture of something called the admissibility program.

01:36.220 --> 01:38.960
Calling it ambitious might be a massive understatement.

01:39.500 --> 01:45.000
It is a full-scale attempt to remap, well, fundamental physics, how human cognition works,

01:45.360 --> 01:50.520
computer architecture, and even the structural reasons why human civilizations collapse.

01:50.720 --> 01:55.400
I mean, it sounds like a theory of everything, which, I have to admit, usually makes my skeptical alarm bells go off.

01:55.400 --> 01:57.720
Oh, for sure. It's a huge red flag normally.

01:57.820 --> 02:03.920
Right. Whenever someone claims they've solved quantum gravity, the human brain, and the fall of Rome in one single document,

02:04.200 --> 02:06.980
you have to wonder if they've just completely lost the plot.

02:07.120 --> 02:09.440
It sounds like crackpot territory at first glance.

02:09.760 --> 02:16.780
It really does. But the deeper I read into this, the more the underlying logic started to warp how I was looking at the room around me.

02:17.320 --> 02:19.520
So let's start at the absolute foundation.

02:20.080 --> 02:23.380
What is the core principle of this admissibility program?

02:23.380 --> 02:26.100
Like, what is the lens we are supposed to be looking through?

02:26.600 --> 02:30.620
So the central shift is moving away from a universe made of static objects.

02:30.760 --> 02:30.980
Okay.

02:31.660 --> 02:38.720
The traditional view, I mean, the one we navigate our daily lives with, is that reality is composed of nouns, things.

02:39.120 --> 02:41.680
You know, a chair, a planet, an electron.

02:41.920 --> 02:42.180
Right.

02:42.520 --> 02:47.720
But this program argues that reality is fundamentally made of what it calls admissible transformations.

02:48.000 --> 02:49.100
Admissible transformation.

02:49.100 --> 03:01.380
Yeah. The universe isn't a collection of objects sitting in empty space. It is a space of what is reachable or possible from any given point. It's a universe of processes and trajectories.

03:01.560 --> 03:07.060
I want to spend a lot of time on that because internalizing that idea completely rewrites how you look at the physical world.

03:07.060 --> 03:12.620
But before we get into the heavy physics and, like, the cosmology of it all, I want to talk about where this actually came from.

03:12.780 --> 03:14.080
The origin story is fascinating.

03:14.440 --> 03:23.720
Because you'd think an intellectual architecture that attempts to redefine gravity started with a bunch of theoretical physicists standing around a chalkboard, you know, doing algebraic geometry.

03:24.560 --> 03:25.480
But it did it.

03:25.480 --> 03:26.180
Far from it.

03:26.180 --> 03:33.880
The oldest, most foundational layer of this entire research program began in the physical, practical world.

03:34.400 --> 03:39.800
It was born out of construction, electrical work, plumbing, and property rehabilitation.

03:40.340 --> 03:42.280
I really struggled with this part at first.

03:42.580 --> 03:51.780
You're telling me a framework that attempts to explain the cosmological smoothing of the early universe was literally inspired by fixing leaky pipes.

03:51.780 --> 03:52.860
I know, it sounds absurd.

03:52.960 --> 03:56.340
I mean, physics is supposed to be about pristine universal laws.

03:56.780 --> 03:59.740
Plumbing is just patching things up in a basement.

03:59.740 --> 04:04.940
But that tension you're feeling is exactly the point the document makes about embodied knowledge.

04:05.080 --> 04:06.220
Okay, unpack that for me.

04:06.640 --> 04:10.500
Well, theoretical physics historically loves to start with a blank slate.

04:11.400 --> 04:18.180
They imagine, you know, a perfectly spherical cow in a frictionless vacuum, and then they write beautiful symmetrical equations about it.

04:18.200 --> 04:19.560
Right, the ideal scenario.

04:20.000 --> 04:20.540
Exactly.

04:20.540 --> 04:30.300
But when you are standing in a hundred-year-old house with a wrench, tasked with fixing a degraded plumbing system, you do not have a blank slate.

04:30.640 --> 04:31.780
No, definitely not.

04:32.000 --> 04:35.460
You can't just erase the house and build the mathematically ideal pipe system?

04:35.540 --> 04:40.520
Right, because if I knock out a load-bearing wall to put in the perfect pipe, the roof collapses.

04:41.020 --> 04:44.040
I'm constrained by the reality of the mess right in front of me.

04:44.340 --> 04:46.440
And that constraint is everything.

04:46.440 --> 04:51.100
You have to ask a fundamentally different question than the theoretical physicist asks.

04:51.520 --> 04:59.500
You look at the system and ask, what transformations are actually available from this specific state right now without causing a catastrophic failure?

04:59.620 --> 05:02.000
You are navigating an admissibility landscape.

05:02.000 --> 05:02.820
Precisely.

05:02.820 --> 05:09.640
And from that sustained, very messy engagement with physical repair, the author extracted four recurring intuitions.

05:10.100 --> 05:14.180
And these four principles formed the bedrock of the entire admissibility program.

05:14.180 --> 05:16.040
Let's break down those four intuitions.

05:16.140 --> 05:17.220
I have them here in my notes.

05:17.360 --> 05:18.960
First is process over object.

05:19.340 --> 05:21.340
Second, accessibility over location.

05:21.640 --> 05:21.840
Right.

05:22.020 --> 05:23.380
Third, repair over construction.

05:23.600 --> 05:25.680
And fourth, continuity over replacement.

05:25.680 --> 05:34.960
If you can just keep those four ideas in your head, the rest of the extremely dense math in this document becomes much, much easier to parse.

05:35.300 --> 05:38.400
Let me test an analogy on you to make sure I'm really getting this.

05:38.480 --> 05:39.880
Let's look at a game of chess.

05:40.020 --> 05:40.900
Okay, great example.

05:40.900 --> 05:47.160
If you look at reality the traditional way, you are looking at the wooden pieces on the board, the objects.

05:47.760 --> 05:49.040
You say, that is a knight.

05:49.300 --> 05:50.220
It is made of oak.

05:50.500 --> 05:51.880
It sits on square G1.

05:52.040 --> 05:52.580
The nouns.

05:52.740 --> 05:53.420
Right, the nouns.

05:53.420 --> 05:59.520
But if you look at reality through the lens of the admissibility program, you aren't looking at the wooden pieces at all, are you?

05:59.640 --> 06:01.300
No, you're looking at the negative space.

06:01.440 --> 06:01.820
Exactly.

06:02.160 --> 06:05.400
You are only looking at the invisible map of legal moves.

06:05.500 --> 06:06.940
You are looking at the reachability.

06:07.560 --> 06:10.720
The reality of the knight isn't the carved piece of wood.

06:11.260 --> 06:15.620
The reality of the knight is the L-shaped trajectory it is permitted to take.

06:15.760 --> 06:17.600
It's defined by what it can do next.

06:17.660 --> 06:17.860
Right.

06:18.260 --> 06:20.140
The game isn't the physical pieces.

06:20.140 --> 06:23.860
The game is the shifting web of admissible transformations.

06:24.360 --> 06:31.160
The physical piece is really just a convenient placeholder for the human eye to track a set of allowed behaviors.

06:31.520 --> 06:35.640
And for anyone listening right now, you can apply this to your own life.

06:35.760 --> 06:36.440
I was so...

06:36.440 --> 06:39.640
Think about how often we are sold the fantasy of the blank slate.

06:39.640 --> 06:47.740
We want to tear down our old habits or our old careers or our old societal structures and build a perfect new one from scratch.

06:47.980 --> 06:48.620
Oh, constantly.

06:48.880 --> 06:50.840
The whole, you know, new year, new me thing.

06:50.920 --> 06:51.320
Exactly.

06:51.800 --> 06:56.980
But the messy reality of living is that replacement is incredibly rare.

06:57.620 --> 07:04.000
We almost always have to focus on repair and maintenance to keep our lives functioning while we try to change them.

07:04.000 --> 07:08.120
That's a profound way to look at it, because we have this cultural obsession with the new.

07:08.680 --> 07:12.500
We view the need for repair as, like, a failure state.

07:12.600 --> 07:15.600
If something needs fixing, we think it's broken or obsolete.

07:16.120 --> 07:19.180
But in the admissibility program, needing repair is not a sign of failure.

07:19.740 --> 07:21.840
Repair is the fundamental category of existence.

07:22.280 --> 07:22.640
Wow.

07:22.980 --> 07:29.100
If something exists in this universe, it requires continuous repair against entropy just to maintain its shape.

07:29.100 --> 07:38.800
So if we take this plumber's intuition, that verbs matter more than nouns, and that the map of legal moves is more real than the wooden chess pieces,

07:39.440 --> 07:43.000
how does that actually translate into a scientific philosophy?

07:43.560 --> 07:47.000
It translates into what the document calls the reachability ontology.

07:47.380 --> 07:49.640
This is the philosophical engine of the whole project.

07:49.740 --> 07:49.900
Okay.

07:50.020 --> 07:51.260
The reachability ontology.

07:51.440 --> 07:51.620
Yeah.

07:51.800 --> 07:55.700
It formally claims that reality is not composed of static entities.

07:55.700 --> 08:00.460
It's composed entirely of reachable trajectories and accessible futures.

08:00.760 --> 08:04.820
See, I need to push back here, because my brain is actively resisting this.

08:04.860 --> 08:07.260
Like, I'm looking at the coffee mug on the desk in front of me.

08:07.380 --> 08:07.600
Okay.

08:07.800 --> 08:08.780
It's solid ceramic.

08:08.980 --> 08:09.720
It has mass.

08:09.920 --> 08:10.740
It reflects light.

08:10.840 --> 08:12.300
It is very clearly a thing.

08:12.500 --> 08:13.300
I can pick it up.

08:13.540 --> 08:15.240
Are you telling me this mug is a verb?

08:15.420 --> 08:18.160
The text has a very specific term for what you're holding.

08:18.400 --> 08:20.540
It calls your mug a frozen process.

08:20.780 --> 08:21.720
A frozen process?

08:22.020 --> 08:22.180
Yeah.

08:22.760 --> 08:24.460
Think about the time frame of your perception.

08:24.460 --> 08:30.180
To a fruit fly, a human being standing still might look like a mountain, you know, a permanent

08:30.180 --> 08:31.100
geographic feature.

08:31.180 --> 08:32.360
Because their lives are so short.

08:32.680 --> 08:32.960
Right.

08:33.240 --> 08:34.980
And to you, that mug looks permanent.

08:35.700 --> 08:40.040
But if you zoom in on a microscopic level, those ceramic molecules are vibrating.

08:40.220 --> 08:41.640
They're exchanging heat with the air.

08:42.000 --> 08:43.260
They are slowly degrading.

08:43.700 --> 08:47.900
Over a long enough timeline, the mug shatters or it erodes into dust.

08:48.060 --> 08:50.380
So it is moving just incredibly slowly.

08:50.740 --> 08:51.220
Exactly.

08:51.220 --> 08:57.580
What you are calling an object is just a trajectory that has become locally stationary relative

08:57.580 --> 08:59.680
to your incredibly narrow slice of time.

08:59.940 --> 09:05.460
It's an accessibility region that has persisted just long enough to look solid to a human nervous

09:05.460 --> 09:05.840
system.

09:06.020 --> 09:09.020
So it's more like a whirlpool in a river.

09:09.280 --> 09:11.420
A whirlpool looks like a distinct object.

09:11.700 --> 09:13.800
I can point at it and say, there is the whirlpool.

09:13.920 --> 09:14.880
I can measure its width.

09:15.160 --> 09:15.380
Yes.

09:15.460 --> 09:16.740
But the whirlpool isn't a thing.

09:16.820 --> 09:18.120
It's a pattern of flowing water.

09:18.120 --> 09:20.760
It's a process that has achieved a temporary stability.

09:21.240 --> 09:24.100
And if the flow of the river changes, the whirlpool vanishes.

09:24.760 --> 09:29.340
Its identity is completely dependent on the continuous flow of the system.

09:29.600 --> 09:29.720
Right.

09:30.220 --> 09:33.840
In this ontology, identity is replaced by trajectory continuity.

09:34.780 --> 09:39.380
The mug isn't defined by its inherent mugness or the specific atoms currently making it up

09:39.380 --> 09:41.260
because those atoms are always cycling in and out.

09:41.520 --> 09:44.060
It's defined by its continuous event history.

09:44.460 --> 09:44.840
Exactly.

09:44.840 --> 09:47.680
And what futures are reachable from its current state.

09:48.260 --> 09:52.880
The document actually cites a couple of essays on this concept with fantastic titles.

09:53.480 --> 09:55.360
One is called The Secret Life of Nouns.

09:55.660 --> 09:58.500
And the other is Verbs Masquerading as Nouns.

09:58.660 --> 10:00.600
Verbs Masquerading as Nouns.

10:00.640 --> 10:01.260
I love that.

10:01.560 --> 10:06.100
So if everything is just a verb masquerading as a noun, then the primary question we ask

10:06.100 --> 10:07.580
in science has to completely change.

10:07.940 --> 10:10.700
We can't ask, what exists here anymore?

10:11.020 --> 10:14.640
Now, the defining question becomes, what can be reached from here?

10:14.640 --> 10:18.120
And that completely alters how we understand space and distance.

10:18.540 --> 10:18.660
Right.

10:18.840 --> 10:22.480
Because distance is no longer just taking a tape measure and stretching it between two

10:22.480 --> 10:22.880
points.

10:23.100 --> 10:25.760
The text talks about reachability distance.

10:26.220 --> 10:28.400
Explain how that differs from physical distance.

10:28.640 --> 10:29.800
Think of a thick glass wall.

10:30.500 --> 10:34.520
You are standing on one side and there is an apple and a table on the other side.

10:34.660 --> 10:34.880
Okay.

10:34.940 --> 10:35.580
I'm picturing it.

10:35.720 --> 10:36.880
Physically, spatially.

10:37.340 --> 10:39.600
That apple might be 12 inches away from your hand.

10:39.600 --> 10:43.340
In a traditional metric space, the distance is incredibly short.

10:43.920 --> 10:48.160
But in terms of reachability distance, that apple might as well be on the moon.

10:48.480 --> 10:53.740
Because there is no admissible transformation, like no legal move for my hand to pass through

10:53.740 --> 10:54.920
the solid glass.

10:54.920 --> 10:55.360
Exactly.

10:56.080 --> 11:03.100
In the reachability ontology, spatial proximity is an illusion if there is no continuous allowable

11:03.100 --> 11:04.320
path connecting the states.

11:04.480 --> 11:08.640
You have to walk all the way down the hall, out the door, around the building, and into

11:08.640 --> 11:09.760
the other room to get the apple.

11:10.220 --> 11:14.620
So the reachability distance is massive, even if the spatial distance is tiny.

11:14.940 --> 11:15.380
Precisely.

11:15.500 --> 11:15.680
Okay.

11:16.040 --> 11:17.840
Conceptually, philosophically, I am with you.

11:17.880 --> 11:19.300
It's a beautiful way to look at the world.

11:19.300 --> 11:24.060
But you can't just tell a physicist, hey, distance is just a vibe based on what you can

11:24.060 --> 11:27.020
reach, and expect them to rewrite quantum mechanics.

11:27.280 --> 11:31.680
How does this philosophy actually formalize into workable physics?

11:32.060 --> 11:35.760
This is where the document shifts from philosophy to its primary theories.

11:36.180 --> 11:41.220
To replace Newtonian space and quantum mechanics, it introduces a continuous physical model called

11:41.220 --> 11:42.280
RSVP.

11:42.760 --> 11:43.360
RSVP.

11:43.740 --> 11:46.020
The Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum.

11:46.120 --> 11:46.620
That's the one.

11:46.620 --> 11:51.920
Let's break this down, because this is where the document gets deep into the weeds of cosmological

11:51.920 --> 11:52.880
field theory.

11:53.840 --> 11:58.720
If the universe isn't made of empty space with little billiard ball particles bouncing

11:58.720 --> 12:01.000
around in it, what is it made of?

12:01.460 --> 12:03.540
RSVP claims the universe is a plenum.

12:04.240 --> 12:06.160
A plenum means it is completely full.

12:06.700 --> 12:08.280
There is no empty void anywhere.

12:08.840 --> 12:12.180
And this fullness is defined by a field theoretic triple.

12:12.500 --> 12:13.700
A field theoretic triple.

12:13.700 --> 12:18.180
So three continuous, interacting fields that dictate everything that happens.

12:18.780 --> 12:19.580
Walk me through them.

12:19.660 --> 12:21.160
What are we actually visualizing here?

12:21.340 --> 12:25.720
The first is a scalar concentration field, denoted by the Greek letter phi.

12:26.360 --> 12:29.980
A scalar field just assigns a single value to every point in space.

12:30.100 --> 12:31.460
Okay, give me an analogy for that.

12:31.740 --> 12:33.380
Think of it like a temperature map of a room.

12:33.720 --> 12:37.360
Every single point in the room has a specific value of concentration or temperature.

12:37.620 --> 12:39.120
Okay, so that's the underlying terrain.

12:39.260 --> 12:40.360
What's the second field?

12:40.360 --> 12:45.560
The second is a vector flow field, denoted by V. If the scalar field is the temperature,

12:46.000 --> 12:50.540
the vector field is the wind. It has both a magnitude and a direction at every point.

12:50.880 --> 12:55.720
It describes the flow, the movement, the trajectories through that scalar terrain.

12:55.820 --> 12:56.880
Got it. And the third?

12:57.080 --> 13:02.500
The third is an entropy density field, denoted by S. This maps the irreversibility and the

13:02.500 --> 13:07.500
dissipation of energy in the system. It dictates the arrow of time, essentially, by showing

13:07.500 --> 13:10.900
where constraints are breaking down and where the system is losing its structure.

13:11.220 --> 13:16.540
So instead of a universe of isolated objects, we have this infinite roiling ocean of concentration

13:16.540 --> 13:20.120
gradients, directional flows, and entropy dissipation.

13:20.240 --> 13:20.680
Exactly.

13:20.840 --> 13:25.120
And everything that we think of as an event or a physical interaction is just a ripple

13:25.120 --> 13:27.820
or a shift in these three continuous fields.

13:27.820 --> 13:33.460
Yes. And because they're using this fluid, reachability-based model, they have to reinterpret

13:33.460 --> 13:39.180
the fundamental forces. Gravity, for example, is no longer just mass-warping space-time like

13:39.180 --> 13:40.700
a bowling ball on a trampoline.

13:40.960 --> 13:45.840
Right. That's the classic Einstein visualization. How does RSVP explain gravity, then?

13:46.400 --> 13:50.940
RSVP describes gravity as descent through admissibility space.

13:50.940 --> 13:56.200
I'm going to need you to unpack descent through admissibility space, because that sounds more

13:56.200 --> 13:58.220
like a poem than a gravitational theory.

13:58.540 --> 14:03.800
Think about water flowing down a mountain. It doesn't choose a path, right? It naturally

14:03.800 --> 14:08.440
follows the gradient of least resistance, seeking a state of lower potential energy.

14:08.660 --> 14:08.960
Okay.

14:09.440 --> 14:15.700
In RSVP, physical systems naturally move towards states that resolve constraints or offer higher

14:15.700 --> 14:20.920
reachability. Gravity isn't a pulling force. It's the natural geometric flow that's

14:20.940 --> 14:25.020
low of the vector field, seeking to resolve gradients in the scalar field.

14:25.440 --> 14:29.580
So things fall because that is simply the most admissible transformation available to them

14:29.580 --> 14:30.620
in that local topography.

14:30.760 --> 14:33.260
Exactly. It's the path of least resistance in the field.

14:33.520 --> 14:37.680
And does this field theory hold up when we look at the really big picture, like the origin

14:37.680 --> 14:43.760
of the universe? Modern physics leans heavily on this idea of cosmic inflation, that right

14:43.760 --> 14:48.500
after the Big Bang, the universe expanded faster than the speed of light for a fraction

14:48.500 --> 14:48.940
of a second.

14:49.040 --> 14:50.300
Right. The inflationary epoch.

14:50.300 --> 14:55.100
Yeah. And it's supposedly the only way physicists can explain why the universe looks so smooth

14:55.100 --> 14:58.820
and uniform in every direction today. Does RSVP use inflation?

14:59.300 --> 15:05.020
It explicitly throws it out. The admissibility program argues that cosmic inflation is a patch.

15:05.580 --> 15:09.620
It's a mathematical hack to fix a broken model of static objects.

15:09.880 --> 15:11.520
Wow. Okay. So what replaces it?

15:11.720 --> 15:17.880
Instead, RSVP explains cosmological smoothing through a mechanism called lamfordine relaxation.

15:17.880 --> 15:21.520
Lamfordine relaxation. That is quite a term.

15:21.520 --> 15:28.240
It sounds complex, but the mechanism is incredibly elegant. In RSVP, the early universe was a highly

15:28.240 --> 15:33.800
constrained, highly tangled field state. Lamfordine relaxation is just the natural process of

15:33.800 --> 15:36.580
constraint erosion and differential entropy descent.

15:36.580 --> 15:42.660
As the entropy field interacts with the flow field, it naturally smooths out extreme gradients over time.

15:42.840 --> 15:55.760
So it's like having a crumpled piece of silk, and instead of pulling the edges out at warp speed to make it perfectly flat, which would be inflation, you just let gravity and the natural weight of the fabric slowly pull the wrinkles out over time.

15:55.760 --> 15:57.280
That is a brilliant visualization.

15:57.620 --> 15:57.760
Yes.

15:58.080 --> 16:07.380
And out of that natural relaxation process, localized areas of coherence emerge. Those coherent areas are what we eventually call galaxies, stars, and planets.

16:07.600 --> 16:16.800
Okay. I think I can visualize RSVP. I can see this giant, infinite landscape of fields flowing and relaxing. But here's the massive problem.

16:17.020 --> 16:17.480
Which is?

16:17.480 --> 16:26.200
If the universe is actually this continuous, infinite, deeply entangled ocean of fields, how on earth do we exist inside it?

16:26.320 --> 16:40.020
Like, how do you or I or even a computer navigate an infinite flow? Because if my brain had to process the infinite complexities of the vector and scalar fields of just the room I'm sitting in, I would short circuit instantly.

16:40.240 --> 16:47.120
This is the exact problem that requires the second primary theory in the document. RSVP describes the universe, but CLIO describes the observer.

16:47.120 --> 16:50.240
CLIO, constraint leveraged inference and optimization.

16:50.580 --> 17:01.360
Precisely. CLIO is all about what happens when a finite observer encounters an infinite continuous space. Because we are finite, we simply cannot ingest the whole field. It is mathematically impossible.

17:01.460 --> 17:03.980
Right. Our hard drives aren't big enough, so to speak.

17:04.280 --> 17:13.700
Exactly. We have to compress reality in order to survive it. We have to project the infinite dimensional universe into a lower dimensional representation.

17:13.700 --> 17:18.760
The document has a really stark phrase for this. It says, abstraction is reduction.

17:19.120 --> 17:32.340
And it means it literally. Every time you describe something, every time you perceive something, you are losing information. You are taking a vastly complex knot of admissibility flows and collapsing it into a single manageable surface value.

17:32.340 --> 17:42.940
So abstraction isn't just a clever mental trick humans do. It is a physical thermodynamic necessity for a finite entity to operate in this universe.

17:43.320 --> 17:49.980
Yes. If you don't compress the data, you can't act. CLIO uses a lot of deep mathematical proofs to formalize this.

17:50.060 --> 17:50.860
Like what kind of math?

17:50.860 --> 18:02.460
It relies on lambda calculus, the Curry-Howard correspondence, and null convention logic to mathematically prove that observers must collapse continuous states into discrete symbols just to compute their next move.

18:02.780 --> 18:08.320
I am not going to pretend I can follow the lambda calculus of it all. But conceptually, I get it. We have to simplify.

18:09.000 --> 18:15.920
But the document draws a very sharp, almost moral line between two different types of this simplification.

18:16.140 --> 18:16.540
It does.

18:16.540 --> 18:22.920
It talks about ethical abstraction versus extraction. Why does it use the word ethical for a mathematical projection?

18:23.420 --> 18:29.020
It uses ethical because the way you choose to compress reality has profound structural consequences.

18:30.100 --> 18:34.900
Ethical abstraction is an act of observation that acknowledges its own incompleteness.

18:35.020 --> 18:36.760
Meaning it knows it's leaving stuff out.

18:36.900 --> 18:41.780
Right. It builds a simplified model, but it leaves hooks back to the underlying complexity.

18:41.780 --> 18:44.740
It respects the original constraints of the system.

18:45.380 --> 18:50.320
And crucially, it allows the underlying reality to veto the model if the model is wrong.

18:50.860 --> 18:52.900
So ethical abstraction knows it's just a map.

18:53.160 --> 18:59.040
And if the map says a bridge is there, but the real world says the bridge is out, the map is willing to be updated.

18:59.400 --> 19:04.260
Exactly. And the pathological opposite of that is what CLIO calls extraction.

19:04.260 --> 19:10.580
Extraction is when you mistake your compressed, low-dimensional projection for the actual reality.

19:11.020 --> 19:17.180
You prioritize the efficiency of your model over the structural constraints of the system you are modeling.

19:17.620 --> 19:19.980
You just throw away the underlying context entirely.

19:20.780 --> 19:23.480
Let me try a really concrete analogy here to see if I've got this.

19:23.560 --> 19:23.960
Go for it.

19:23.960 --> 19:31.660
Imagine I have an incredibly complex 3D mechanical pocket watch, hundreds of gears, all interacting.

19:31.780 --> 19:34.500
Now, I want to understand it, so I take a 2D photograph of it.

19:35.400 --> 19:36.860
Taking the photo is an abstraction.

19:37.240 --> 19:39.420
It's flat. It's efficient. I can put it in my pocket.

19:39.620 --> 19:43.220
Right. And if you acknowledge it's just a photo, that's ethical abstraction.

19:43.220 --> 19:47.580
Right. But extraction would be if I take that 2D photo and I give it to an engineer and say,

19:47.820 --> 19:50.860
build me this machine using only this photo as your guide.

19:51.040 --> 19:51.540
Oh, that's good.

19:51.540 --> 19:57.420
And the engineer tries to lay out all the gears completely flat on a table, exactly like the picture.

19:57.660 --> 20:00.820
And then we both stare at it, wondering why the gears won't turn.

20:01.400 --> 20:07.360
We destroyed the complex 3D constraints of the watch just to enjoy the efficiency of the 2D image.

20:07.740 --> 20:10.060
That captures the danger of extraction perfectly.

20:10.420 --> 20:16.260
When you extract, you lose the depth, the reachability that made the system function in the first place.

20:16.360 --> 20:18.140
And when that happens, the system breaks down.

20:18.240 --> 20:20.500
It does. You cause structural damage to the system.

20:20.500 --> 20:23.120
Which brings us right back to the plumber, actually.

20:23.560 --> 20:29.340
Because when you break the constraints of a system through extraction, the only way forward is repair.

20:29.980 --> 20:33.900
Okay. We are building a really fascinating architecture here.

20:34.160 --> 20:38.380
We have RSVP, which gives us the continuous physics of the universe.

20:38.380 --> 20:45.180
We have CLIO, which explains how finite observers have to compress that continuous universe into manageable models.

20:45.500 --> 20:45.560
Right.

20:45.860 --> 20:47.460
But I'm still feeling a disconnect.

20:47.780 --> 20:48.140
Why that?

20:48.660 --> 20:52.640
Because RSVP and CLIO both deal with fields and projections.

20:52.640 --> 20:54.160
It's all very smooth and continuous.

20:54.700 --> 20:56.500
But my life doesn't feel continuous.

20:56.500 --> 20:59.620
It feels like a series of discrete, irreversible events.

20:59.760 --> 21:00.480
Okay, I see what you mean.

21:00.480 --> 21:01.320
I woke up.

21:01.780 --> 21:03.420
I chose to make coffee instead of tea.

21:03.900 --> 21:04.700
I signed a contract.

21:05.020 --> 21:05.980
I said no to a meeting.

21:06.280 --> 21:07.220
These are hard borders.

21:07.580 --> 21:12.560
How does a continuous field theory account for the hard, irreversible events of an actual life?

21:12.820 --> 21:18.880
To solve that, the admissibility program introduces its third primary theory, which is called SPHERPOP.

21:19.380 --> 21:19.900
SPHERPOP.

21:19.900 --> 21:26.540
I have to admit, amidst all this heavy academic terminology, SPHERPOP sounds like a brand of chewing gum.

21:26.640 --> 21:27.420
It really does.

21:27.600 --> 21:32.640
It is a disarmingly playful name for an incredibly rigorous formal system, though.

21:32.740 --> 21:33.360
So what is it?

21:33.820 --> 21:37.200
SPHERPOP is the event-theoretic sibling to RSVP.

21:37.900 --> 21:43.340
If RSVP handles the smooth fields, SPHERPOP handles discrete events.

21:43.860 --> 21:48.120
It maps reality not through gradients, but through categorical transitions.

21:48.120 --> 21:49.760
Like what kind of transitions?

21:49.900 --> 21:56.800
Its fundamental primitives are things like formation, collapse, binding, refusal, and the residues left behind by those actions.

21:56.980 --> 22:03.500
And the most radical claim SPHERPOP makes, the sentence that really made me stop reading and just stare at the wall for a minute, is this.

22:03.940 --> 22:05.160
History is identity.

22:05.520 --> 22:09.260
We touched on this with the coffee mug earlier, but SPHERPOP formalizes it.

22:09.860 --> 22:12.200
You are not defined by the substance you are made of.

22:12.260 --> 22:13.500
You are not a collection of atoms.

22:13.740 --> 22:17.160
Identity is constituted by the irreversible event history of a system.

22:17.160 --> 22:19.560
So to use the classic ship of Theseus paradox.

22:19.560 --> 22:20.060
Oh, perfect.

22:20.280 --> 22:26.360
If you replace every single board on a wooden ship over 10 years, is it the same ship?

22:26.860 --> 22:28.300
SPHERPOP says yes, absolutely.

22:28.700 --> 22:31.180
Because the identity of the ship isn't in the wood.

22:31.460 --> 22:35.880
It's in the continuous, unbroken event history of its maintenance and existence.

22:35.880 --> 22:36.400
Yes.

22:36.960 --> 22:44.860
The discrete transitions, the replacing of a board, the sailing of a voyage, these events permanently alter the set of accessible futures.

22:45.460 --> 22:50.320
That specific sequence of closed doors and open pathways is the identity.

22:50.680 --> 22:54.540
The document also mentions that in SPHERPOP, time is forkable.

22:55.020 --> 22:55.620
What does that mean?

22:55.660 --> 22:57.740
Are we talking about like the sci-fi multiverse?

22:57.740 --> 23:01.080
Not quite the sci-fi multiverse, but rather computational pathways.

23:01.700 --> 23:07.260
A system can maintain multiple diverging execution histories simultaneously as it calculates its next state.

23:07.580 --> 23:11.400
It uses what the text calls a meld operator to eventually reconcile them.

23:11.660 --> 23:13.780
Okay, so it explores options before committing.

23:13.940 --> 23:14.100
Right.

23:14.200 --> 23:16.740
But the key to SPHERPOP is the concept of structured irreversibility.

23:17.380 --> 23:19.440
Meaning some things just cannot be undone.

23:19.440 --> 23:20.040
Exactly.

23:20.540 --> 23:23.680
And it's not because you, the observer, are flawed or like a time machine.

23:24.140 --> 23:28.160
The irreversibility is a fundamental geometric feature of the system.

23:28.840 --> 23:33.400
Once a collapse event occurs, the admissibility landscape is permanently altered.

23:33.900 --> 23:36.200
You cannot simply walk backwards down the path.

23:36.320 --> 23:39.540
Here is where the math of the Flyxion map apparently pulled off a miracle.

23:40.160 --> 23:43.760
Because for a long time, the authors were stuck on an open question.

23:43.760 --> 23:53.240
How do you mathematically connect the discrete, irreversible jumps of SPHERPOP with the smooth, continuous gradients of RSVP?

23:53.700 --> 23:56.360
They seem like two completely incompatible frameworks.

23:56.700 --> 23:58.360
It is the classic tension in physics.

23:59.320 --> 24:04.580
How do you marry the discrete, quantized nature of reality with the continuous relativity of space-time?

24:05.380 --> 24:07.520
And the document highlights a massive breakthrough here.

24:07.620 --> 24:11.420
They formulated a mathematical proof involving a category theory functor.

24:11.420 --> 24:14.120
A functor. Okay, I'm going to need you to translate that into English.

24:14.260 --> 24:14.420
Sure.

24:15.460 --> 24:23.680
In category theory, a functor is a map that translates between two entirely different mathematical categories while preserving their internal structure.

24:24.240 --> 24:29.500
The authors proved a specific functor called F, which maps SPHERPOP to RSVP.

24:30.080 --> 24:33.100
They write it as F colon SP to RSVP.

24:33.120 --> 24:36.340
So it's essentially a translation engine between the two realities.

24:36.340 --> 24:50.580
Yes. It mathematically proves the discrete constraint accumulation, so the events, the choices, the bindings of SPHERPOP, can be mapped smoothly into the continuous field coherence redistribution of RSVP.

24:50.760 --> 24:51.240
Wow.

24:51.580 --> 25:00.340
It shows that discrete events are the source of irreversible commitments, and the continuous fields of RSVP are the target landscape where those commitments actually play out physically.

25:00.340 --> 25:06.560
It bridges the gap between a discrete choice and the physical universe's reaction to that choice.

25:06.880 --> 25:09.860
That is staggering. They essentially proved the physics of making a decision.

25:09.980 --> 25:10.320
It did.

25:10.740 --> 25:12.860
Which leads me to something I really want to focus on.

25:12.980 --> 25:23.000
Amidst all these event primitives in SPHERPOP, like formation, collapse, binding, the document spends a lot of time on one specific primitive, the autonomy of refusal.

25:23.260 --> 25:27.400
Saying no. It is one of the most vital concepts in the entire architecture.

25:27.400 --> 25:35.100
Because in most behavioral economics or utility theory or even AI training, saying no isn't treated as a primary action.

25:35.340 --> 25:40.120
It's treated as a negative space. It's just a failure to maximize utility or a change in preference.

25:40.740 --> 25:45.260
But SPHERPOP argues that refusal is an autonomous, foundational primitive.

25:45.520 --> 25:47.940
And that completely changes how we model behavior.

25:48.780 --> 25:52.880
Refusal cannot be reduced to a math equation of utility maximization.

25:52.880 --> 25:59.300
When a system refuses an input or a binding, it is exercising an active geometric constraint.

25:59.980 --> 26:02.640
Think about what this means for you, listener, in your daily life.

26:02.900 --> 26:10.660
When you say no to a project at work, or you set a boundary in a relationship, or you refuse to engage in an argument, you often feel guilty.

26:10.880 --> 26:14.520
Very often. You feel like you're failing to optimize or failing to be productive.

26:14.740 --> 26:19.000
Right. But this physics framework completely validates that experience.

26:19.000 --> 26:23.320
It absolutely does. Saying no isn't a glitch in your productivity.

26:24.020 --> 26:27.860
It is you establishing a fundamental geometric boundary in your own event history.

26:28.320 --> 26:31.520
You are permanently altering the topology of your reachable future.

26:31.820 --> 26:35.000
Exactly. You are protecting the constraints of your identity.

26:35.940 --> 26:38.940
Refusal has its own autonomous formal properties.

26:39.580 --> 26:44.760
And without it, a system just dissolves into the entropy of whatever the environment demands of it.

26:44.760 --> 26:48.320
You literally shape the geometry of your life by what you refuse.

26:48.620 --> 26:51.720
I think that might be the most empowering thing I've ever read in a physics paper.

26:51.920 --> 26:53.400
It's deeply resonant, isn't it?

26:53.740 --> 26:57.940
So let's stack this up, because we are climbing higher and higher up this architecture.

26:58.260 --> 27:00.960
We have RSVP giving us the continuous fields.

27:01.340 --> 27:03.760
We have CIO giving us the finite abstractions.

27:04.700 --> 27:09.200
We have SpherePop giving us the discrete events and the event history of identity.

27:09.620 --> 27:13.140
What happens when the admissibility program smashes all three of these together?

27:13.140 --> 27:14.380
With the resulting entity.

27:14.500 --> 27:15.320
Yes, exactly.

27:15.640 --> 27:19.880
When you synthesize those systems, you arrive at layers three and four of the architecture.

27:20.280 --> 27:25.740
You get a completely new definition of intelligence, formalized as active geodesic inference, or AGI,

27:26.100 --> 27:30.400
and its implementation into a cognitive architecture known as HYDRA.

27:30.880 --> 27:32.380
Active geodesic inference.

27:33.060 --> 27:38.280
I know AGI almost universally stands for artificial general intelligence right now in the tech world,

27:38.280 --> 27:43.120
but here they are co-opting the acronym for something entirely different.

27:43.320 --> 27:49.780
And they are doing it purposefully to draw a stark contrast with how the tech industry currently views intelligence.

27:50.140 --> 27:54.300
The dominant paradigm right now, the one driving all the massive large language models,

27:54.520 --> 27:57.060
is that intelligence is fundamentally about optimization.

27:57.460 --> 27:57.700
Right.

27:57.700 --> 28:01.900
You give the AI a massive data set, you set a goal, you define a loss function,

28:02.080 --> 28:09.900
and the AI's entire intelligence is just finding the fastest, most efficient way to minimize that loss to get the desired output.

28:10.160 --> 28:11.880
It's just greedy optimization.

28:12.340 --> 28:15.480
Active geodesic inference explicitly rejects that model.

28:15.960 --> 28:18.700
Under AGI, intelligence is not optimization.

28:18.980 --> 28:19.500
Then what is it?

28:19.840 --> 28:24.640
Intelligence is defined as the capacity to remain within dynamically admissible low-action histories.

28:24.640 --> 28:27.600
Okay, translate remaining within dynamically admissible histories.

28:28.060 --> 28:30.520
What does an intelligent entity actually do?

28:30.800 --> 28:34.160
It means true intelligence is about keeping your future options open.

28:35.300 --> 28:40.100
An intelligent system actively reshapes its environment and its own configuration space

28:40.100 --> 28:45.160
to ensure it doesn't back itself into a corner where no further transformations are possible.

28:45.240 --> 28:46.540
So it's playing the long game.

28:46.800 --> 28:47.200
Exactly.

28:47.780 --> 28:50.800
It isn't trying to greedily optimize for a short-term reward

28:50.800 --> 28:55.760
if that reward requires destroying the structural constraints it needs to survive tomorrow.

28:56.380 --> 29:01.160
So a highly optimized system might solve the maze faster than anything else,

29:01.320 --> 29:04.180
but it might burn the maze down to do it.

29:04.720 --> 29:10.280
A truly intelligent system navigates the maze in a way that ensures it can still navigate the maze tomorrow.

29:10.500 --> 29:11.780
That is a perfect way to put it.

29:12.280 --> 29:13.940
Optimization is often extraction.

29:14.840 --> 29:17.220
Intelligence is continuous repair and navigation.

29:17.420 --> 29:18.420
That makes so much sense.

29:18.420 --> 29:23.140
And to explain how an intelligent mind actually processes information internally to achieve this,

29:23.520 --> 29:27.500
AGI introduces a fascinating concept called semantic isomers.

29:27.660 --> 29:28.700
Semantic isomers.

29:28.780 --> 29:31.880
This is where the cognitive science of this document really shines.

29:32.260 --> 29:32.980
Break this down for me.

29:33.200 --> 29:34.220
Let's borrow from chemistry.

29:35.120 --> 29:40.240
In chemistry, isomers are molecules that have the exact same chemical formula,

29:40.600 --> 29:41.840
so the same number of atoms.

29:42.080 --> 29:45.320
But those atoms are arranged in different physical structures.

29:45.580 --> 29:45.840
Okay.

29:45.840 --> 29:48.380
Because the structure is different, the molecules behave differently,

29:48.560 --> 29:50.840
even though they are made of the exact same stuff.

29:51.100 --> 29:51.320
Okay.

29:51.400 --> 29:54.240
So how does that apply to human or artificial thought?

29:54.620 --> 29:58.160
AGI proposes that reasoning itself has semantic isomers.

29:58.780 --> 30:03.040
It means there are distinct internal reasoning topologies,

30:03.160 --> 30:05.060
so different geometric paths of thought,

30:05.060 --> 30:08.580
that can produce the exact same external answer.

30:08.580 --> 30:12.000
I want to make sure I really understand this, so let me try an analogy from a classroom.

30:12.180 --> 30:12.560
Please do.

30:12.940 --> 30:15.100
Imagine two students taking a math test.

30:15.960 --> 30:18.180
They are given a complex calculus problem.

30:18.540 --> 30:23.760
Student A is highly visual, so they internally solve the problem using geometric visualization.

30:24.440 --> 30:28.680
Student B is purely analytical, so they crunch through the algebraic equations.

30:28.940 --> 30:29.200
Okay.

30:29.420 --> 30:32.820
They both write down the exact same current answer on the paper, 42.

30:33.280 --> 30:35.100
So externally their outputs are identical.

30:35.100 --> 30:35.580
Right.

30:35.980 --> 30:39.920
But internally the architecture of how they arrived at that answer,

30:40.020 --> 30:42.700
the actual paths they walked through their admissibility landscape,

30:42.880 --> 30:44.960
was structurally completely different.

30:45.540 --> 30:48.900
Their thought processes are semantic isomers of each other.

30:49.080 --> 30:51.220
That is exactly what the theory describes.

30:51.400 --> 30:51.520
Yeah.

30:51.600 --> 30:53.740
And you might wonder, why does this matter?

30:54.120 --> 30:56.660
Who cares how they got there if the answer is the same?

30:56.760 --> 30:56.940
Right.

30:57.020 --> 30:57.680
Why does it matter?

30:57.680 --> 31:03.180
It matters enormously because it explains one of the most frustrating roadblocks researchers are

31:03.180 --> 31:08.540
currently hitting in artificial intelligence development today, specifically when they try

31:08.540 --> 31:10.080
to perform model distillation.

31:10.460 --> 31:11.560
Model distillation.

31:11.880 --> 31:17.640
This is when tech companies take a massive, expensive AI model, and they try to compress it

31:17.640 --> 31:22.740
into a smaller, cheaper model by essentially averaging out the weights of how the big model behaves.

31:22.920 --> 31:23.200
Correct.

31:23.400 --> 31:25.960
They want the performance without the computational cost.

31:25.960 --> 31:30.700
But what they are finding is that when you distill a reasoning model, the performance often

31:30.700 --> 31:31.700
falls off a cliff.

31:32.260 --> 31:35.640
The distilled model starts producing complete logical garbage.

31:36.020 --> 31:37.800
And AGI explains why.

31:38.240 --> 31:43.800
Because if we go back to our two math students, if you take student A's geometric brain and

31:43.800 --> 31:48.360
student B's algebraic brain, and you try to average them together to create a smaller,

31:48.520 --> 31:50.380
distilled, super student...

31:50.380 --> 31:51.340
You don't get a genius.

31:51.340 --> 31:51.780
No.

31:52.080 --> 31:53.760
You get a frustrated phase transition.

31:54.040 --> 31:54.880
You get nonsense.

31:55.360 --> 31:59.400
You cannot mathematically average a geometric shape with an algebraic formula.

31:59.620 --> 32:01.600
That's because they are semantic isomers.

32:01.740 --> 32:06.200
Their internal structures are entirely incompatible, even if their outputs align.

32:06.880 --> 32:12.160
When AI companies try to average out billions of different semantic isomers inside a neural network,

32:12.560 --> 32:14.920
they destroy the coherence of the internal logic.

32:14.920 --> 32:19.680
The path you take to an answer is structurally real and cannot be extracted without breaking

32:19.680 --> 32:19.960
it.

32:20.320 --> 32:20.640
Precisely.

32:20.880 --> 32:24.260
Which goes all the way back to the danger of extraction we talked about in CLIO.

32:24.480 --> 32:24.660
Yeah.

32:24.660 --> 32:26.800
You destroy the constraint for the sake of efficiency.

32:27.080 --> 32:27.320
Right.

32:27.600 --> 32:30.980
And to solve this, the admissibility program introduces HYDRA.

32:31.980 --> 32:38.240
HYDRA is the grand cognitive architecture that attempts to run RSVP, CLIO, SpherePOP, and

32:38.240 --> 32:41.060
a Weez Mechanical Memory Framework all simultaneously.

32:41.320 --> 32:42.740
That sounds incredibly complex.

32:42.740 --> 32:43.820
It is.

32:44.240 --> 32:49.620
It models a mind that doesn't just optimize, but actively infers, makes discrete choices,

32:49.940 --> 32:54.760
and navigates a continuous landscape without destroying its internal semantic topologies.

32:55.040 --> 32:57.580
It is an incredibly beautiful way to model a mind.

32:58.400 --> 33:01.400
But the Flyxion map doesn't stop at the individual brain.

33:01.720 --> 33:06.940
Because once you solve the theory of cognition, the authors pan out to a macro level.

33:06.980 --> 33:07.860
They zune all the way out.

33:08.040 --> 33:08.300
Yeah.

33:08.300 --> 33:13.520
They ask, if history is identity, how does history actually survive time?

33:13.880 --> 33:19.480
And why do huge collections of minds, human civilizations, inevitably rise and collapse?

33:19.720 --> 33:21.860
This brings us to the applied frameworks in Layer 4.

33:22.640 --> 33:25.580
Preservation, ECHFRI, and civilization geometry.

33:25.580 --> 33:27.480
Let's focus on memory first.

33:28.000 --> 33:31.640
Because the common assumption is that if you write something down, or you carve it in stone,

33:31.700 --> 33:33.720
or you save it to a hard drive, it is remembered.

33:33.960 --> 33:35.060
You have preserved it.

33:35.140 --> 33:41.200
But the framework draws a very sharp, crucial distinction between physical persistence and reachability.

33:41.740 --> 33:45.800
Just because an object survives physically does not mean it is remembered by the system.

33:45.920 --> 33:46.760
Give me an example of that.

33:46.760 --> 33:49.840
Think of a floppy disk from 1995 sitting in your basement.

33:50.620 --> 33:52.000
The physical disk persists.

33:52.660 --> 33:55.860
The magnetic states encoding the data are still perfectly intact.

33:56.200 --> 33:57.460
It has physically survived.

33:57.600 --> 33:57.860
Okay.

33:58.380 --> 34:00.580
But you do not have a floppy disk drive anymore.

34:01.360 --> 34:04.860
The software to read those files hasn't existed for 20 years.

34:05.540 --> 34:08.440
The information on that disk is completely unreachable.

34:08.600 --> 34:11.000
It persists, but it is no longer admissible.

34:11.000 --> 34:11.600
Exactly.

34:12.160 --> 34:18.320
For a pattern to be truly remembered, it has to cross what the document calls the ECHFORIC activation threshold.

34:18.520 --> 34:20.100
ECHFORIC activation threshold.

34:20.220 --> 34:20.460
Yes.

34:21.020 --> 34:25.860
ECHFRI is the process of a stored trace being brought back into active interaction with a queue.

34:26.700 --> 34:28.560
Memory isn't a passive storage vault.

34:29.120 --> 34:32.180
Memory is the active capacity for retrieval and integration.

34:32.440 --> 34:35.760
So if it cannot be integrated into the current admissibility landscape,

34:36.060 --> 34:39.100
it is effectively dead, regardless of its physical persistence.

34:39.440 --> 34:39.900
That's right.

34:39.900 --> 34:44.720
And when you scale this concept up to the level of society, we get into civilization geometry.

34:45.540 --> 34:47.700
And this is where the plumber returns in full force.

34:47.860 --> 34:49.320
Repair theory at the macro level.

34:49.680 --> 34:49.940
Yes.

